Process

    1. Virtual Box
        1.1 Type 2 hypervisors are virtualbox and vmware workstation. In the course virtualbox is favored since the it has a lot more features compared to the free version of wmware.
        1.2 Fast forward 2026, wmware workstation pro is free for personel use and that means there are no restrictions on it. So it could be used as well.
    2. Virtual Box Connectivity
        2.1 VM's can be started in a couple of modes: detached, headless and normal. In normal mode an additional monitor pops up and it is just as showing the physical screen of a computer.
        2.2 In headless mode VM starts without monitor and it can only be reached through ssh or rdp.
        2.3 In detachable mode, VM starts with monitor but the monitor can be closed without closing down the VM.
    3. Virtual Box Networking
        3.1 NAT Network
            I. A LAN is created inside the VM and each VM can access to other networks through the IP address of host machine.
        3.2 NAT
            I. Each VM can access to other networks through the IP address of host machine but not to each other. More isolation.
        3.3 Bridge Network
            I. A LAN network of VM is created and it is as if they are connected/bridged to the physical LAN of the host machine. So they get ip addresses from the same subnet as the host machine.
        3.4 Host only
            I. A LAN is created inside the VM, VM's can only talk with each other and with the host machine
        3.5 Internal network
            I. A LAN is created inside the VM, VM's can only talk with each other. Not even with the host machine.
        3.5 Internet connectivity
            I. NAT: Possible
            II. Bridge: Possible
            III. Host only: Possible with ip forwarding
            IV. Internal network: Not possible
